1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:08,497
Welcome back! Last time, we worked out the two rules light follows near a black hole, and wrote a program that traces rays with them.

2
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:12,145
But all we got was a diagram. Lines on a flat page.

3
00:00:12,667 --> 00:00:22,189
Today we fix that. By the end of this video, you'll be flying a camera around a black hole, in real time, and seeing what you'd actually see if you were there.

4
00:00:22,700 --> 00:00:32,134
To pull that off, we need three new things. A camera. A ray of light for every single pixel. And a way to run our physics on the graphics card.

5
00:00:33,966 --> 00:00:41,574
So how does a normal ray tracer make a picture? There's a camera, and in front of it, a grid of pixels. That's your screen.

6
00:00:42,066 --> 00:00:47,630
For every pixel, we shoot a ray from the camera, through that pixel, and ask: what does it hit?

7
00:00:48,099 --> 00:00:54,514
Yes, that's backwards from how real light travels. But it's clever: we only bother with the rays that actually reach the camera.

8
00:00:54,999 --> 00:01:04,695
In normal space, light goes in straight lines. So a ray is just a start point plus a direction, and hitting a sphere is one quadratic equation. Easy.

9
00:01:05,166 --> 00:01:08,012
Now swap the sphere for a black hole.

10
00:01:08,499 --> 00:01:12,664
And the rays bend. Some swing around the back. Some never come out.

11
00:01:13,133 --> 00:01:20,186
There's no neat formula for these paths. To find out where a ray ends up, we have to walk along it, one small step at a time.

12
00:01:20,666 --> 00:01:25,551
The good news? We already know how to take a step. It's the two rules from last time.

13
00:01:27,133 --> 00:01:32,572
But one ray per pixel adds up fast. Even a small window has about a million pixels.

14
00:01:33,066 --> 00:01:35,615
Each ray needs a hundred steps or more.

15
00:01:36,100 --> 00:01:40,683
And for smooth motion, we want sixty pictures every second.

16
00:01:41,166 --> 00:01:45,495
Do the maths. That's billions of Runge-Kutta steps, every single second.

17
00:01:46,966 --> 00:01:50,995
A processor grinds through them a few at a time. Way too slow.

18
00:01:51,466 --> 00:02:00,693
A graphics card is a different beast. It has thousands of small cores, and it runs the same little program on every pixel, all at once.

19
00:02:01,166 --> 00:02:09,583
And you've already met that little program. It's the fragment shader! Last time, all it did was return a colour. This time, it does the physics.

20
00:02:11,166 --> 00:02:20,690
One catch: a fragment shader only runs where something is being drawn. So we draw one shape that covers the entire screen: a single, oversized triangle.

21
00:02:21,166 --> 00:02:28,265
Its vertex shader doesn't even need a buffer of points. It makes up the three corners from the vertex number: zero, one, and two.

22
00:02:28,733 --> 00:02:36,517
They land outside the screen, which only goes from minus one to one. OpenGL trims off the extra, and what's left is exactly the screen. Neat trick, right?

23
00:02:37,599 --> 00:02:44,360
On the C plus plus side, that's an empty vertex array, and one draw call with three vertices.

24
00:02:44,833 --> 00:02:52,142
From now on, the fragment shader runs once for every pixel in the window. Whatever colour it returns, that's what you see.

25
00:02:53,733 --> 00:02:59,258
Now the physics has to move out of C plus plus and into the shader. Here's our derivatives function from last time.

26
00:02:59,733 --> 00:03:01,428
And here's the shader version.

27
00:03:01,900 --> 00:03:09,680
Look how little changes! Vec two becomes vec three. And the outputs are marked with the word out, instead of being references.

28
00:03:10,166 --> 00:03:12,715
The body? Identical. Line for line.

29
00:03:13,200 --> 00:03:21,741
And that's not luck. Our two rules never said how many dimensions there are. They're written with vectors and dot products, so three dimensions comes for free.

30
00:03:22,233 --> 00:03:27,587
Photon energy and the Runge-Kutta step move over in exactly the same way.

31
00:03:29,166 --> 00:03:36,603
Next up: the camera. It circles the hole, and we describe where it is with two angles and a distance. Those are what your mouse changes.

32
00:03:37,099 --> 00:03:42,658
Every frame, the program turns those into a position, with sines and cosines.

33
00:03:43,133 --> 00:03:47,345
Then it needs three directions. Forward points from the camera to the hole.

34
00:03:47,833 --> 00:03:57,231
Right has to be perpendicular to forward, and to the vertical. And a cross product gives you exactly that: a vector at right angles to two others.

35
00:03:57,699 --> 00:04:00,204
And up is perpendicular to both of those.

36
00:04:00,699 --> 00:04:08,477
These get sent to the shader as uniforms. Remember: a uniform is a value the program sends to the shader, the same for every pixel.

37
00:04:08,966 --> 00:04:15,940
So when you drag the mouse, the angles change, the uniforms change, and the next frame gets traced from the new spot.

38
00:04:17,533 --> 00:04:24,974
Now we're inside the fragment shader. First, each pixel works out where it sits on the screen, from minus one to plus one.

39
00:04:25,466 --> 00:04:32,010
Its ray starts at the camera. And its direction is forward, plus a bit of right and a bit of up, depending on where the pixel is.

40
00:04:32,499 --> 00:04:40,579
That direction is the ray's momentum, p. And photon energy gives us p t, exactly like in stage one.

41
00:04:41,066 --> 00:04:51,226
One honest confession: this treats space as flat right where the camera sits. That's a fine approximation, as long as the camera keeps its distance from the hole.

42
00:04:52,799 --> 00:04:59,090
Then comes the same loop as trace ray from last time, with one difference. Instead of saving a path, it picks a colour.

43
00:04:59,566 --> 00:05:05,305
Inside the horizon? No light comes out of there. This pixel is black. Done.

44
00:05:05,799 --> 00:05:09,492
Far away? The ray escaped. Stop stepping.

45
00:05:09,966 --> 00:05:13,531
Otherwise, take one Runge-Kutta step, and go around again.

46
00:05:14,566 --> 00:05:23,361
After the loop, we ask: which way is the escaped ray heading now? Rule one gives us its velocity. Then we look up that direction in the sky.

47
00:05:24,932 --> 00:05:31,097
The sky function turns a direction into a colour. It can paint stars.

48
00:05:31,566 --> 00:05:41,601
Or it can paint a grid, like the lines of latitude and longitude on a globe, with the top half blue and the bottom half red. That makes the bending so much easier to see.

49
00:05:43,899 --> 00:05:50,190
And here it is! That black disc in the middle is the shadow. Every ray aimed in there fell into the hole.

50
00:05:50,699 --> 00:05:57,375
Its edge is exactly the critical aim we found last time: five point two M. Way bigger than the hole itself.

51
00:05:57,899 --> 00:06:08,952
Around it, the grid gets stretched and folded. And just outside the shadow, you can see the entire sky a second time, squeezed into a thin ring. That light went all the way around the hole.

52
00:06:09,466 --> 00:06:17,412
And check out the colours. The top of the sky is blue, right? But there's blue below the hole. Those rays got bent right over the top.

53
00:06:17,932 --> 00:06:25,452
Swap in the stars, and it looks like this. A million rays, a few hundred steps each, re-traced every single frame.

54
00:06:25,966 --> 00:06:35,617
Still, a black hole on its own looks a bit empty. Next time, we give it something to light it up: a disk of hot gas, orbiting at half the speed of light. Don't miss that one.

